/symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-tool\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"M14.7 6.3a4 4 0 0 0-5.4 5.4L3 18v3h3l6.3-6.3a4 4 0 0 0 5.4-5.4l-2.5 2.5-2.5-2.5z\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-clip\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><rect x=\"5\" y=\"4\" width=\"14\" height=\"17\" rx=\"2\"/><path d=\"M9 4V3h6v1M9 10h6M9 14h6M9 18h3\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-check\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"m5 12.5 4.5 4.5L19 7.5\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-minus\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"M6 12h12\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-info\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"9\"/><path d=\"M12 11v6M12 7.5v.5\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-star\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"M12 2.5l2.9 6.1 6.6.8-4.9 4.6 1.3 6.6L12 17.3l-5.9 3.3 1.3-6.6-4.9-4.6 6.6-.8z\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-down\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"M12 4v12M6 10l6 6 6-6M5 20h14\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-cert\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><rect x=\"3\" y=\"4\" width=\"18\" height=\"12\" rx=\"2\"/><path d=\"M7 8h10M7 11h6M15 16v5l2-1.5 2 1.5v-5\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-family\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><circle cx=\"7\" cy=\"6\" r=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"17\" cy=\"6\" r=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"2\"/><path d=\"M3 20c0-4 2-7 4-7s4 3 4 7M13 20c0-4 2-7 4-7s4 3 4 7\"/></symbol>\n</svg>\n\n", "header": "<header class=\"site-head\">\n    <a class=\"logo\" href=\"/en\" aria-label=\"Ocean Way, home\"><svg viewBox=\"2 2 116 116\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><defs><clipPath id=\"logo-clip\"><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"60\" r=\"47.01\"/></clipPath></defs><g clip-path=\"url(#logo-clip)\" fill=\"currentColor\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"72.3\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"54.6\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"11\"/><path d=\"M28 59 C36 36 72 23.5 118 32 L118 51 C84 41 47 43.5 28 59Z\"/></g><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"60\" r=\"52.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"11\"/></svg></a>\n    <nav class=\"site-nav\" aria-label=\"Main\">\n      <a class=\"navl\" href=\"/en#courses\">Courses</a>\n      <a class=\"navl\" href=\"/en#passages\">Passages</a>\n      <a class=\"navl on\" href=\"/en/theory\">Theory</a>\n      <a class=\"navl\" href=\"/en#fleet\">Fleet</a>\n      <a class=\"navl\" href=\"#\">Prices</a>\n    </nav>\n    <div class=\"site-tools\">\n      <details class=\"lang\" translate=\"no\"><summary class=\"round\" aria-label=\"Language: English\">EN</summary><ul class=\"lang-menu\"><li><a href=\"/en/theory/weather\" hreflang=\"en\" lang=\"en\" aria-current=\"true\">English<span>EN</span></a></li><li><a href=\"/ru/theory/weather\" hreflang=\"ru\" lang=\"ru\">Русский<span>RU</span></a></li><li><a href=\"/de/theory/weather\" hreflang=\"de\" lang=\"de\">Deutsch<span>DE</span></a></li><li><a href=\"/es/theory/weather\" hreflang=\"es\" lang=\"es\">Español<span>ES</span></a></li></ul></details>\n      \n      \n      \n      \n      \n      \n      \n      \n      <button class=\"round\" type=\"button\" id=\"themeBtn\" aria-label=\"Theme\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-theme\"/></svg></button>\n      <button class=\"round\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Saved\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-heart\"/></svg></button>\n      <button class=\"round\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Account\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-user\"/></svg></button>\n    </div>\n  </header>", "main": "\n    <article>\n      <header class=\"art-head\">\n        <nav class=\"crumbs\" aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\"><ol><li><a href=\"/en\">Home</a></li><li><a href=\"/en/theory\">Theory</a></li><li aria-current=\"page\">Weather and navigation</li></ol></nav>\n        <h1 class=\"display\">Reading the weather: Beaufort, forecasts, meltemi</h1>\n        <p class=\"lead\">Good skippers decide most of their day before they leave the berth. Here is how to read the Beaufort scale and a forecast, know the Mediterranean's local winds, and make a calm go or no-go call.</p>\n        <div class=\"byline\"><span class=\"meta\" style=\"color:var(--ink)\">Ocean Way instructors</span><span class=\"meta\">Updated 1 Nov 2026</span><span class=\"meta\">6 min read</span><span class=\"lvl\">Bareboat skipper</span></div>\n      </header>\n      <div class=\"art-grid\">\n        <div class=\"art-body\">\n          <details class=\"art-mobile-toc band\"><summary>On this page<svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-chev\"/></svg></summary><ol class=\"toc-list\"><li><a href=\"#beaufort\">The Beaufort scale</a></li><li><a href=\"#forecasts\">Where to get a forecast</a></li><li><a href=\"#reading\">Reading the forecast</a></li><li><a href=\"#local-winds\">Local winds</a></li><li><a href=\"#reefing\">Reef early</a></li><li><a href=\"#go-no-go\">Go or no go</a></li><li><a href=\"#practise\">Practise it on the water</a></li></ol></details>\n          <div class=\"band summary\"><span class=\"kicker\">In short</span><ul><li>Forecast wind is the mean; plan and reef for the gusts.</li><li>Use several sources: national services, NAVTEX, VHF and grib apps.</li><li>The meltemi builds through the day, so sail early.</li><li>If in doubt, wait in harbour.</li></ul></div>\n          <section id=\"beaufort\">\n<h2>The Beaufort scale</h2>\n<p>The Beaufort scale describes wind strength in \"forces\" from 0 to 12. Forecasts in Greece, Turkey and Croatia often give wind in Beaufort, while apps usually show knots (nautical miles per hour). You need to switch between the two without thinking.</p>\n<p>The table covers the range a charter skipper meets in a normal season. The knots are the mean (average) wind, not the gusts. The last column is a rough guide for a typical 40-foot (12-metre) cruising yacht with a sensible crew. Your own limits depend on the boat, the crew and the sea.</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Force</th><th>Knots</th><th>Sea</th><th>On a 40 ft yacht</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>0</td><td>Under 1</td><td>Flat, like a mirror</td><td>Motor</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>1–3</td><td>Ripples</td><td>Motor, or drift slowly</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>4–6</td><td>Small wavelets</td><td>Light sailing, full sail</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>7–10</td><td>Large wavelets, a few white crests</td><td>Easy sailing, full sail</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4</td><td>11–16</td><td>Small waves, frequent white horses</td><td>Good sailing; heeling, ready to reef</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5</td><td>17–21</td><td>Moderate waves, many white horses, some spray</td><td>First reef, smaller headsail upwind</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6</td><td>22–27</td><td>Large waves, foam crests everywhere, spray</td><td>Two reefs; hard work upwind</td></tr>\n<tr><td>7</td><td>28–33</td><td>Sea heaps up, foam blown in streaks</td><td>Deep reefs; stay in harbour if you can</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8</td><td>34–40</td><td>Moderately high waves, spray blown off crests</td><td>Gale; do not set out</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The sea state for a given force also depends on fetch (the distance the wind has blown over open water) and on how long it has been blowing. A Force 6 in a sheltered bay is a very different thing from a Force 6 after two days across the open Aegean.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"forecasts\">\n<h2>Where to get a forecast</h2>\n<p>Use more than one source and compare them. If they disagree, plan for the stronger one.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>National weather services.</b> The Hellenic National Meteorological Service (Greece), the Turkish State Meteorological Service and the Croatian Meteorological and Hydrological Service all publish marine forecasts by sea area, usually with English versions. These are the official forecasts, and they carry the gale warnings.</li>\n<li><b>NAVTEX.</b> A small receiver on board that prints or displays safety messages, including weather forecasts and gale warnings, from coastal stations. The international service on 518 kHz is in English. You select the stations for your area.</li>\n<li><b>VHF coast stations.</b> Coast radio stations announce weather broadcasts on channel 16, then read them on a working channel. In Croatia there are continuous recorded forecasts on dedicated VHF channels. The pilot book (the sailing guide for the area) lists the channels and times.</li>\n<li><b>Grib-based apps.</b> A GRIB file is raw output from a computer weather model, shown as arrows or colours on a map. Apps are quick and visual, and you can compare several models. But models smooth out local effects, so they often under-read the wind near capes, in channels and below high land.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"reading\">\n<h2>Reading the forecast</h2>\n<h3>Wind arrows</h3>\n<p>Wind is always named after the direction it comes <em>from</em>. A north wind blows from the north towards the south. On most apps the arrow flies with the wind, so it points to where the wind is going. Some apps use wind barbs instead: the shaft points into the wind, and each long feather is 10 knots, each short one 5 knots.</p>\n<h3>Mean wind and gusts</h3>\n<p>The headline wind speed is the mean wind, averaged over about ten minutes. Gusts are short bursts above that. In the Mediterranean summer, gusts are often a third to a half stronger than the mean, and more in the lee of high islands. A forecast of \"18 knots, gusts 28\" means you should rig the boat for 28.</p>\n<p>Look at the trend through the day, not just one number. Check the time the wind is expected to build and the time it should ease, and plan your passage around them.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"local-winds\">\n<h2>Local winds</h2>\n<h3>Meltemi</h3>\n<p>The meltemi is the summer wind of the Aegean. It blows from roughly May to September, strongest and most regular in July and August. It comes from the north-east in the north Aegean, from the north in the Cyclades, and turns north-west towards the Dodecanese and the south-west coast of Turkey.</p>\n<p>It often starts light in the morning, builds towards midday and peaks in the afternoon. It may ease in the evening, but in a strong spell it can blow Force 6–7 day and night for several days. It comes with clear blue skies, so there are no clouds to warn you. Experienced Aegean skippers sail early, before it fills in, and choose routes that keep the wind behind the beam (off the side or from behind).</p>\n<h3>Sirocco</h3>\n<p>The sirocco is a warm south to south-east wind from North Africa. By the time it reaches Greece, Italy or Croatia it is humid and hazy, sometimes carrying reddish dust. In the Adriatic it is called the <em>jugo</em>. It usually builds over a day or more, and the long fetch raises a big, long swell. It is more common in spring and autumn.</p>\n<h3>Bora</h3>\n<p>The bora (<em>bura</em> in Croatian) is a cold north-east wind in the Adriatic that falls down the coastal mountains. It is very gusty and can arrive within minutes, sometimes from a clear sky. In the worst places, such as the Velebit Channel, gusts can exceed 50 knots. It is mostly a winter wind but can appear in summer. A cap of cloud sitting on the mountain tops is a classic warning sign. Croatian forecasts name the bora directly, so read them every morning.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"reefing\">\n<h2>Reef early</h2>\n<p>Reefing means making the sails smaller. The old rule holds: the first time you wonder whether to reef, reef. A reef put in early is calm and easy. A reef put in late, with the boat over on her side and the crew tired, is how things break and people get hurt.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the forecast is Force 5 or more, reef before you leave harbour. You can always shake it out.</li>\n<li>Most charter yachts have a furling mainsail and a roller genoa (the large front sail). Ease the sheet to take the load off before you roll a sail in.</li>\n<li>Reef for the gusts, not the mean wind.</li>\n<li>Before rounding a cape or entering a channel, reduce sail first. Wind accelerates there.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"go-no-go\">\n<h2>Go or no go</h2>\n<p>Before each passage, the skipper makes a clear decision, and says it out loud to the crew. Work through these questions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>What is the mean wind and the gust forecast, and when does it peak?</li>\n<li>Which direction is it from? Will you be beating (sailing towards the wind) or running with it?</li>\n<li>Are there exposed stretches, capes or channels on the route?</li>\n<li>Is the crew rested, experienced and not seasick? Is the boat in good order?</li>\n<li>Is the destination sheltered from this wind? What is your plan B if conditions get worse?</li>\n<li>Does your charter agreement set any wind limits?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If the honest answer to any of these worries you, wait. Waiting a day in harbour costs very little. Many good skippers simply leave at dawn and are tied up before the afternoon wind arrives.</p>\n<aside class=\"note warn\"><b>Watch out</b><p>High islands make their own gusts. In a meltemi, wind pours down the lee (sheltered) side of islands such as Andros, Tinos or Evia and hits the water in violent, shifting gusts, often much stronger than the forecast. The water close in may look calm. Watch for dark patches racing across the surface, reef before you get there, and give high headlands a wide berth.</p></aside>\n</section>\n<section id=\"practise\">\n<h2>Practise it on the water</h2>\n<p>On our courses you read the morning forecast from several sources, compare them and make the go or no-go call yourself. You practise reefing in harbour first, then under way as the wind builds, until it becomes routine.</p>\n</section>\n\n          <div class=\"art-mobile-course\"><div class=\"band art-course\"><span class=\"kicker\">Practise it on</span><div class=\"card-title\">International Bareboat Skipper</div><p>Learn this on the water with an instructor, in a week living aboard.</p><a class=\"pill pill-primary pill-wide pill-sm\" href=\"/en/courses/bareboat-skipper\">See the course</a></div></div>\n        </div>\n        <aside class=\"art-side\" aria-label=\"On this page\"><span class=\"kicker\">On this page</span><ol class=\"toc-list\"><li><a href=\"#beaufort\">The Beaufort scale</a></li><li><a href=\"#forecasts\">Where to get a forecast</a></li><li><a href=\"#reading\">Reading the forecast</a></li><li><a href=\"#local-winds\">Local winds</a></li><li><a href=\"#reefing\">Reef early</a></li><li><a href=\"#go-no-go\">Go or no go</a></li><li><a href=\"#practise\">Practise it on the water</a></li></ol><div class=\"band art-course\"><span class=\"kicker\">Practise it on</span><div class=\"card-title\">International Bareboat Skipper</div><p>Learn this on the water with an instructor, in a week living aboard.</p><a class=\"pill pill-primary pill-wide pill-sm\" href=\"/en/courses/bareboat-skipper\">See the course</a></div></aside>\n      </div>\n    </article>\n    <section class=\"sec\" style=\"margin-top:96px\">\n      <div class=\"sec-head row-head\"><div><span class=\"kicker\">Theory</span><h2 class=\"h2\" style=\"margin-top:12px\">Keep reading</h2></div><div class=\"row-nav\"><a class=\"pill pill-quiet\" href=\"/en/theory\">All theory</a></div></div>\n      <div class=\"hscroll\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"More theory\"><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/position-fixing\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1534008897995-27a23e859048?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"Boats in a bay between cliffs\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Coastal skipper</span><span class=\"card-title\">Where are we? Position fixing and dead reckoning</span><span class=\"card-meta\">How to fix a yacht's position on a paper chart: latitude and longitude, dead reckoning, estimated position, bearing fixes, transits and compass error.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/points-of-sail\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1540946485063-a40da27545f8?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"A sailing yacht close-hauled on open water\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span><span class=\"card-title\">Points of sail: how a yacht sails close to the wind</span><span class=\"card-meta\">Learn the points of sail from close-hauled to running: the no-go zone, sail trim on each point, tacking and gybing, apparent wind and reading telltales.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/knots\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1563296291-14f26f10c20f?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"A schooner with all sails set\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span><span class=\"card-title\">Six knots every crew member uses</span><span class=\"card-meta\">Six sailing knots every crew member needs: bowline, clove hitch, round turn and two half hitches, cleat hitch, figure-of-eight and reef knot, step by step.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/right-of-way\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1605281317010-fe5ffe798166?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"A motor yacht crossing open water\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Bareboat skipper</span><span class=\"card-title\">Right of way at sea: the rules skippers use daily</span><span class=\"card-meta\">The COLREGs a yacht skipper uses daily: lookout, safe speed, risk of collision, give-way rules for sail and power, overtaking, motorsailing and channels.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/navigation-lights\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1534447677768-be436bb09401?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"A yacht under a starry night sky\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Bareboat skipper</span><span class=\"card-title\">Navigation lights and day shapes</span><span class=\"card-meta\">Masthead, side and stern lights, tricolour, anchor light and day shapes under COLREG Part C, plus how to read fishing boats, tows and ships at night.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/med-mooring\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1570077188670-e3a8d69ac5ff?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"A harbour town lit at dusk above the sea\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Bareboat skipper</span><span class=\"card-title\">Mediterranean mooring, step by step</span><span class=\"card-meta\">How to moor stern-to in the Mediterranean with lazy lines or your own anchor: crew briefing, prop walk, the approach, crossed anchors and leaving.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a></div>\n    </section>\n  ", "footer": "<footer class=\"site-foot\">\n    <div class=\"foot-row\">\n      <span class=\"meta\">Ocean Way · Layout prototype. Prices, dates and people are sample content. Photos via Unsplash.</span>\n      <nav class=\"foot-links\"><a href=\"#\">About</a><a href=\"#\">Telegram</a><a href=\"#\">Privacy</a></nav>\n    </div>\n  </footer>", "scripts": ["\nconst root = document.documentElement;\ndocument.getElementById('themeBtn').addEventListener('click', () => { if (root.dataset.theme === 'night') delete root.dataset.theme; else root.dataset.theme = 'night'; });\nif (new URLSearchParams(location.search).get('theme') === 'night') root.dataset.theme = 'night';\n(() => {\n  const links = [...document.querySelectorAll('.art-side .toc-list a')]; if (!links.length) return;\n  const secs = links.map((a) => document.getElementById(a.getAttribute('href').slice(1)));\n  const io = new IntersectionObserver((es) => es.forEach((e) => { if (e.isIntersecting) links.forEach((a, i) => a.classList.toggle('on', secs[i] === e.target)); }), { rootMargin: '-20% 0px -70% 0px' });\n  secs.forEach((s) => s && io.observe(s));\n})();\n"], "tail": "", "styles": "theory"}